<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.11.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01451</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.11.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>aaded099-e708-481b-9231-0dd055625442</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <section class="check-understanding">
      <exercise data-content-id="8e0f0500-55dd-4440-9978-28a0af0a9367" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following:</para><para>Function \(h\) describes the height of a ball, in inches, after \(n\)<emphasis> </emphasis>bounces and is defined
        by the equation \(h(n)=120 \cdot (\frac{4}{5})^n\).</para>
      <para> What is \(h(3)\)? Round to the nearest integer. Think about what it represents in this situation.</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3. Are you using it
        the right way? </para>
          <para>The correct answer is 61. After 3 bounces, the bounce height is about 61 inches. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="6d7401c6-c597-45e1-815d-25b0c4ade80f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Could \(h(n)\) be 150? </para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is no. The ball is dropped from 120 inches, when \(n=0\), and decreases for each bounce
        after that. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="10c85cd5-5b32-428f-922e-29567dcc1617" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Which ball loses its height more quickly, this ball, modeled by \(h(n)=120 \cdot (\frac{4}{5})^n\), or a
          tennis ball whose height in inches after \(n\) bounces is modeled by the function \(f\) where \(f(n)=50 \cdot
          (\frac{5}{9})^n\)?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3. Are you using it
        the right way? </para>
          <para>The correct answer is the ball modeled by \(f(n)\). The ball modeled by \(f(n)\) loses height more quickly
        because the bounce factor is \(\frac59\), which is less than \(\frac45\). </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="e7b5aa97-329f-4653-b67e-535dcc64da64" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>How many bounces would it take before the ball, modeled by \(h(n)\), bounces less than 12 inches from the
          surface?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again. </para>
          <para>The correct answer is 11. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="2f20d8bb-d3db-4e5e-b972-61d20ef47f9a" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>After a second bounce, a ball reached a height of 70 cm. The rebound factor for the ball was 0.8. From
          approximately what height, in cm, was the ball dropped?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>560</para><para>56</para><para>21</para><para>109</para></problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 109 cm</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.4 – 5.11.5. Are you using it
        the right way?</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 109 cm. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-content-id="133a4d47-85ea-4592-98ce-f24d7e3a3aa1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>Using the table below, find the height, in cm, at which the ball was dropped.


        </para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <thead/>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>
              Number of Bounces</emphasis></entry><entry>
              1
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              3
            </entry>
            <entry>
              4
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>Height in cm</emphasis></entry><entry>
              90
            </entry>
            <entry>
              54
            </entry>
            <entry>
              32
            </entry>
            <entry>
              19
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
      </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct!</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.4 – 5.11.5 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.
      </para>
          <para>The correct answer is 150.
      </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </section>
</content>
